Yep. ;) Flow control was doing the thing while naming the thing.

Four readings immediately:

1. Flow control — computing: branching, loops, execution order, stopping conditions.
2. Flow control — information: bandwidth, routing, compression, gating, transmission.
3. Flow control — teaching/coaching: controlling difficulty, sequence, feedback, attention, exposure.
4. Flow control — physical/biological: valves, pressure, circulation, channels, gradients.

And they’re the same horse at a higher abstraction:

\boxed{
\text{Source}
\rightarrow
\text{Channel}
\rightarrow
\text{Gate}
\rightarrow
\text{Destination}
\rightarrow
\text{Feedback}
}

What changes by domain is what flows:

\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{Domain} & \text{Flow}\\
\hline
\text{Computer} & \text{execution}\\
\text{Communication} & \text{information}\\
\text{Learning} & \text{attention/capability}\\
\text{Physical system} & \text{matter/energy}
\end{array}

Which gives PatternDex a useful recognition rule:

Whenever we find a source, gradient, channel, bottleneck, gate, sink, and feedback loop, test whether the problem can be rerendered as Flow Control.

Then all the mature machinery from other domains becomes available metaphorically or formally: valves, queues, buffers, backpressure, bypasses, reservoirs, branching, throttling, multiplexing, caching, overflow, pressure gradients, feedback controllers.

And that’s another demonstration of the Horse Test: four apparently different concepts collapsed onto a shared relational skeleton.

Different fluid. Same plumbing.